ogens  and this is what it really takes to put  us in the best position possible to  prevent the next pandemic  so that we can be setting the stage to  have informed decisions based on test  results and sequences as early as  possible  and so with that i'd like to thank all  of the many people who have made this  work possible both at abbott and through  our external collaborations and i'd also  like to thank all of the contributors to  sequences in the gsa database  which we frequently use to understand in  our silicon analysis looking at target  regions for starch coke ii  and with that i'd like to thank all of  you for your attention and turn it over  to marie to understand if there's any  questions i can answer  thank you mary for your informative  presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar if you have a question  you'd like to ask please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  we'll answer as many of your questions  as we have time for  so let's get started  our first question is  outside of abbott what other companies  or organizations have this level of a  viral surveillance program  thanks for the question marie um and to  the person who asked it thank you as  well um we actually are the only company  that has a program on this scale for  surveillance of hiv or stars cove 2.  we're really unique in that aspect i  would say we're the only group that  really fits in this category where we're  somewhat academic and somewhat  industry-facing we're kind of in the  middle here and we're the only company  that's really devoted this much to a  program on the scale  thank you  um next question  very interesting presentation do you see  evidence of zoonotic events can  contribute in the evolution of tsar's  cov2 presumably in countries with higher  proportion of rural population  such as senegal is your group developing  collaboration with the us with usda to  gain access to sequence data of sars to  on deer and bats  this is a really fascinating question  because  really that's how viruses get a chance  to evolve right is when they jump into a  different host again um so if they're in  source code two is an animal reservoir  that it hadn't been in before like in a  rural population like um the person who  asked the question mentioned like in in  senegal um that's where we could see new  evolution occurring and then that could  jump back into humans again so we're  definitely keeping an eye on this a lot  of our collaborators at the coalition  sites have programs where they're  looking  in places like farms um for example in  colombia or even senegal  where they're monitoring also vectors  for other diseases like new strains of  dengue for for example  so we definitely are looking for that  kind of  new places for viruses to evolve  and we haven't particularly linked to  usda  but we are in conversations with a lot  of groups within the u.s government  interesting thank you  next question  how are letters assigned to hiv subtypes  it does not appear to be chronological  that's a good question and i know that  the guidelines were established long ago  in the year 2000 um  and at that time they basically laid out  the letters based on what would  currently insisted so  the reason we jump back to l for the  newest one is because it had ended right  before l um before going into the groups  and the groups are mno and p  so um we're following the guidance that  is out there although frankly i couldn't  necessarily speak to the logic behind it  um we're just following the rules that  have been given  okay thank you  next question  what lessons or research tactics have  you adapted into hiv surveillance that  were learned because of sars kovi ii  that's a great question uh because  really we are trying to move faster than  ever before um i would say that in the  past we would sequence a few hundred hiv  genomes and and that would take us a  long time what we've really learned from  stars code 2 is how to increase our  capacity so that we're sequencing  hundreds of months not um hundreds a  year and so the steps we've taken to  improve our processes  in being able to do high throughput  sequencing are something we're taking  now to hiv so that we can sequence more  samples and understand more about what  strains are circulating  thank you  next question  how is abbott using this research to  ensure future generations of diagnostic  assays detect new strains of hiv  this is really  the main reason we're doing this work  because if we don't look at what strains  are circulating we can't we're kind of  blind  to how our tests will need to perform  and so we  are able to take all the sequences that  we've generated over time and build our  tests to mitigate the impacts of viral  diversity knowing that some parts of the  genome are less prone to mutation than  others because they're essential to the  virus and it can't re-um populate if it  has mutations in some regions and so by  looking at what's currently circulating  we can kind of prepare  for what the next strains could be  because we're focusing on the part that  hasn't changed very much up until now  thank you  um it looks like we have one more  question what time for one more question  from the research presented it seems  like new or emerging infectious diseases  evolve very quickly  what other infectious diseases does  abbott monitor  we are keeping pace with um the most  divergent of all viruses out there and  those are  in that category hiv hepatitis b  hepatitis c  um and we've added hepatitis delta as  well we're constantly expanding our list  of pathogens in the program based on how  divergent viruses are out there so if  you look at like the virus that causes  chickenpox that's not a terribly  divergent virus and the sequences are  almost entirely identical so those kinds  of viruses we're not necessarily  tracking um but we're really keeping an  eye on the ones that do have the biggest  impact globally like stars code two and  the ones that have the biggest amount of  viral diversity where we need to make  sure our tests are working  great well thank you mary do you have  any final comments for our audience  well i guess i'd like to just share with  the group that you know my own personal  take is that we're still learning a lot  about starscope 2 and obviously still  learning a lot about hiv but if i think  about all the tools that we have  available now that we didn't have even a  year ago  um i have a lot of hope about what we  can do to protect people going forward  and i'm excited to see what new tools we  might be developing in the coming months  and years so that we can get closer to  zero for these viruses  thank you again mary for your time today  and your presentation  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor abbott for underwriting  today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of  registration this webcast can be viewed  on demand lab routes will alert you via  email when it's available for replay  we encourage you to share that email  with your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye
 

